Title: "Special Operator Headers"
Id: "925dd22b-6e86-4411-b0ad-fb25f32a85aa"

Text: "A command header may be an operator form, using a special operator (`+`, `-`, `*`, `:-:`, ...) or a named operator (`|op|`) between placeholder operands. Operator headers most commonly back a `Disambiguates:` group that resolves the notation to a command:

```mlg-fragment
[x_ - y_]
Disambiguates:
when: x_, y_ is \\set
to: x_ \\.set.minus./ y_
else: x_ :-: y_
Documented:
. written: \"x_? - y_?\"
```

Each `when:`/`to:` branch is tried in order, and `else:` is the fallback.

Special operator headers can use any sequence of special operator characters and may include underscore suffixes and trailing primes, such as `*_1`, `*_free`, `*'`, and `*'_a`.

An operator form heading may also back a `Defines:` or `Declares:` item directly, for example:

```mlg-fragment
[x_ \\.natural.+./ y_]
[f(x_)]
[f| x_]
[x_ |f]
```

Standalone placeholder operands are normalized to named callable parameters, so `n_ \\.natural.+./ m_` binds `n` and `m` without separate `when:` declarations. Operator headers do not use the colon-directed forms such as `:|op|`."
Id: "4b616a6a-2192-4345-8f55-eafa451cc2e5"
